warrant_agent/whatif.py:107-110 ranks every waiting version by transitive graph reach, then simulates only
order[:limit]. Line 115 sorts the simulated subset by actual changes. A graph citation is not a change of state or
reliance, so a candidate with many citations can displace the actual maximum. Even limit=12 (NEXT_MAX) cannot recover
that omitted candidate. The MCP tool exposes this via next=true (warrant_agent/mcp_server.py:253-267).

Counterexample at M: 14 acceptances await bob. Thirteen decoy closures each have three open lineages citing their
root, and accepting each decoy changes only its own lineage. A fourteenth closure has one already-accepted dependent
whose guard requires it closed, so accepting the fourteenth changes two lineages. At limits 1, 5 (the default), and
12 (the maximum), next excludes that fourteenth candidate and returns only candidates with changes=1. A direct
whatif of the omitted candidate returns count=2. The fixture's records.jsonl bytes are unchanged after all calls.

